1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,835
Your CTO asks: "You ran the tests,
they passed, you committed.

2
00:00:08,875 --> 00:00:12,787
CI ran that commit and failed
on the line you had fixed.

3
00:00:12,827 --> 00:00:17,374
Which of the two is wrong?" Make
your guess, and say it out loud.

4
00:00:22,214 --> 00:00:27,474
Neither. They tested different things. The
commit contained what was in the index,

5
00:00:27,514 --> 00:00:30,899
and CI, the server that tests
every commit, ran that.

6
00:00:30,939 --> 00:00:34,842
Your own tests ran against
something else: the files on disk.

7
00:00:35,182 --> 00:00:39,945
Git doesn't build a commit from your files.
It builds it from the index, a second

8
00:00:39,985 --> 00:00:44,391
complete record of the project that
sits between the working tree and HEAD.

9
00:00:44,431 --> 00:00:49,253
The working tree is the files on your disk,
and HEAD is your last commit, the last

10
00:00:49,293 --> 00:00:52,691
saved snapshot. Understanding
usually stops at this layer.

11
00:00:53,031 --> 00:00:57,231
The textbook notes that "How do
I undo 'git add' before commit?"

12
00:00:57,271 --> 00:01:02,529
is the fifth most-voted Git question on
Stack Overflow, with 11,626 votes on the

13
00:01:02,569 --> 00:01:08,027
first of October 2026, and that none of the
ten popular beginner resources surveyed

14
00:01:08,067 --> 00:01:11,604
for this course teaches the
index as a data structure.

15
00:01:11,644 --> 00:01:16,439
We will. Later in this video, you'll
reproduce the CTO's puzzle yourself.

16
00:01:18,279 --> 00:01:21,933
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

17
00:01:21,973 --> 00:01:26,959
The next four videos take the index apart.
This one establishes what it is and what

18
00:01:26,999 --> 00:01:28,110
git add does to it.

19
00:01:28,450 --> 00:01:32,802
Hold on to one idea, and I'll repeat
it: the index is not a list of changes.

20
00:01:32,842 --> 00:01:37,425
It's a full snapshot. Everything that
confuses people about staging follows from

21
00:01:37,465 --> 00:01:41,875
getting that sentence wrong, and everything
in these four videos follows from

22
00:01:41,915 --> 00:01:46,614
getting it right. In the picture, the middle
box holds a full copy, like the other

23
00:01:46,654 --> 00:01:46,845
two.

24
00:01:48,685 --> 00:01:50,264
Here's what you'll walk away with.

25
00:01:50,604 --> 00:01:52,016
After this video you can:

26
00:01:52,356 --> 00:01:57,496
Describe the index as a flat list of paths
with blob IDs, and prove that it equals

27
00:01:57,536 --> 00:01:59,707
HEAD's tree when nothing is staged.

28
00:02:00,047 --> 00:02:02,011
Name the two things git add writes.

29
00:02:02,351 --> 00:02:06,115
Show that staging is a snapshot of
the file at the moment of git add.

30
00:02:06,455 --> 00:02:10,610
Explain what remains in the repository
after a staged file is unstaged.

31
00:02:12,450 --> 00:02:17,587
In one sentence. The index is a single
file, .git/index, that lists every tracked

32
00:02:17,627 --> 00:02:22,957
path together with the ID of a blob, and
git commit turns exactly that list into the

33
00:02:22,997 --> 00:02:27,752
tree of the new commit. A blob is the
object that holds one file's content.

34
00:02:27,792 --> 00:02:30,820
A tree is the object that
records one directory.

35
00:02:31,160 --> 00:02:35,718
Precisely. Git's data-model document says:
"The index, also known as the 'staging

36
00:02:35,758 --> 00:02:39,976
area', is a list of files and the
contents of each file, stored as a blob".

37
00:02:40,016 --> 00:02:43,098
And: "Unlike a tree, the index
is a flat list of files.

38
00:02:43,138 --> 00:02:47,867
When you commit, Git converts the list of
files in the index to a directory tree and

39
00:02:47,907 --> 00:02:49,797
uses that tree in the new commit".

40
00:02:50,137 --> 00:02:53,574
Each entry has four fields.
The file type and mode.

41
00:02:53,614 --> 00:02:58,004
The blob ID. A stage number, which
is 0 except during a conflict.

42
00:02:58,044 --> 00:03:03,321
And the full path. Mode, blob ID and path
are what the commit's tree receives.

43
00:03:03,361 --> 00:03:08,366
Each entry also carries bookkeeping
that never leaves your machine: cached

44
00:03:08,406 --> 00:03:11,910
filesystem data, and flag
bits. Video 18 opens both.

45
00:03:11,950 --> 00:03:15,864
The old name "cache" survives
in options such as --cached.

46
00:03:16,204 --> 00:03:20,065
The correction. The definition
corrects the most common wrong model.

47
00:03:20,105 --> 00:03:24,884
Nothing in the index is a "change". "Staged
changes" are the result of comparing the

48
00:03:24,924 --> 00:03:29,473
index with HEAD. And an index with nothing
staged is not empty: it describes the

49
00:03:29,513 --> 00:03:30,506
same tree as HEAD.

50
00:03:30,846 --> 00:03:36,487
Inside .git. One binary file, created by
the first git add: a 12-byte header, the

51
00:03:36,527 --> 00:03:40,696
entries sorted by path, optional
extensions, and a checksum.

52
00:03:41,036 --> 00:03:44,996
What git add writes. In one sentence: git
add <path> stores the current content of

53
00:03:45,036 --> 00:03:48,948
the file as a blob in the object database,
Git's store inside the dot git folder,

54
00:03:48,988 --> 00:03:51,583
and writes that blob's ID
into the path's index entry.

55
00:03:51,623 --> 00:03:54,754
It's a copy taken at that moment,
not a subscription to the file.

56
00:03:55,094 --> 00:03:59,084
The manual: "It only adds the content of
the specified file(s) at the time the add

57
00:03:59,124 --> 00:04:03,212
command is run; if you want subsequent
changes included in the next commit, then you

58
00:04:03,252 --> 00:04:04,392
must run git add again".

59
00:04:04,732 --> 00:04:09,867
Two things are written: one object, unless
an identical blob already exists, and one

60
00:04:09,907 --> 00:04:14,856
index entry. No ref moves, meaning no
branch name, so no reflog, Git's journal of

61
00:04:14,896 --> 00:04:19,969
such moves, records an add. git add is 🟢
SAFE: it writes a blob and an index entry.

62
00:04:20,309 --> 00:04:25,186
The root cause from the hook. A fix that
was tested but is missing from the commit

63
00:04:25,226 --> 00:04:30,223
was edited after the last git add. The commit
is written from the index, which still

64
00:04:30,263 --> 00:04:35,200
held the earlier version. Status showed MM.
Add the file again and commit, and read

65
00:04:35,240 --> 00:04:37,479
git diff --cached before every commit.

66
00:04:37,819 --> 00:04:41,765
What an add leaves behind. Unstaging
removes the entry, not the object.

67
00:04:41,805 --> 00:04:46,201
The blob that git add wrote stays in the
object database as a dangling blob, an

68
00:04:46,241 --> 00:04:50,749
object that nothing refers to. A push,
which sends commits to another repository,

69
00:04:50,789 --> 00:04:55,409
doesn't send it: a push sends what the
pushed commits need, and no commit refers to

70
00:04:55,449 --> 00:04:56,083
this object.

71
00:04:56,423 --> 00:05:01,964
Garbage collection, Git's clean-up of unused
objects, removes the stray object once

72
00:05:02,004 --> 00:05:05,528
it's older than gc.pruneExpire,
two weeks by default.

73
00:05:05,568 --> 00:05:11,109
Until then it's also a rescue route: content
that was staged once and then lost can

74
00:05:11,149 --> 00:05:12,655
be found with git fsck.

75
00:05:12,995 --> 00:05:18,325
Where this matters and where it does not.
This isn't something to "use" or "not

76
00:05:18,365 --> 00:05:23,558
use". It's how every commit is made. The
practical rule is about trust: "What

77
00:05:23,598 --> 00:05:28,520
exactly will be in this commit?" has
one authoritative answer, the index.

78
00:05:28,560 --> 00:05:34,162
Reviewers, CI and every clone receive the
tree that was written from it, never your

79
00:05:34,202 --> 00:05:35,045
working tree.

80
00:05:36,885 --> 00:05:41,695
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
the packing list for the next shipment.

81
00:05:41,735 --> 00:05:46,787
It names every item that will be in the box,
not the items that differ from the last

82
00:05:46,827 --> 00:05:51,880
box, and the warehouse ships what the list
says even if the shelf has changed since.

83
00:05:52,220 --> 00:05:56,432
Use it on the hook. You changed the item
on the shelf after you wrote the list.

84
00:05:56,472 --> 00:05:58,639
The warehouse shipped what the list said.

85
00:05:58,979 --> 00:06:04,241
The analogy breaks in two places. Writing
a line on this list also puts a copy of

86
00:06:04,281 --> 00:06:07,776
the item into storage at that
moment. That's the blob.

87
00:06:07,816 --> 00:06:12,816
And during a conflict, the list can carry
three candidate lines for one item.

88
00:06:12,856 --> 00:06:14,845
That's for video 18 and Part 2.

89
00:06:16,685 --> 00:06:18,146
Now the real list, with real IDs.

90
00:06:18,486 --> 00:06:21,620
On the left, four lines: mode,
blob ID, stage, full path.

91
00:06:21,660 --> 00:06:26,186
A flat list, sorted by path. On the right,
the same information as tree objects: a

92
00:06:26,226 --> 00:06:30,864
top-level tree with one blob and two subtrees.
The blob IDs on the left are the blob

93
00:06:30,904 --> 00:06:35,486
IDs on the right. The arrow is git commit,
or git write-tree: Git converts the flat

94
00:06:35,526 --> 00:06:38,994
list into a directory tree. Nothing
on either side is a change.

95
00:06:40,834 --> 00:06:44,874
git ls-files --stage prints the
four fields of every entry.

96
00:06:44,914 --> 00:06:50,337
The repository is clean: nothing is staged.
Quick quiz: how many lines does the

97
00:06:50,377 --> 00:06:55,316
index have? Zero, because nothing is
staged, or four, one for each file?

98
00:06:55,356 --> 00:06:56,423
Say it out loud.

99
00:07:01,263 --> 00:07:05,092
Four. Four lines with full paths,
although nothing is staged.

100
00:07:05,132 --> 00:07:10,103
If you said zero, that's the list-of-changes
model talking, and almost everyone

101
00:07:10,143 --> 00:07:14,099
starts there. HEAD's tree has
three entries, two of them trees.

102
00:07:14,139 --> 00:07:19,174
Flattened with git ls-tree -r, it gives
the same four modes, blob IDs and paths.

103
00:07:19,514 --> 00:07:23,647
The file itself: the signature DIRC, the
format version, and the entry count, four.

104
00:07:23,987 --> 00:07:29,203
git write-tree 🟢 SAFE does on request what
git commit does every time: it writes the

105
00:07:29,243 --> 00:07:32,895
index out as tree objects and
prints the ID of the top one.

106
00:07:32,935 --> 00:07:36,962
Predict how that ID relates to the
tree of HEAD. Say it out loud.

107
00:07:37,002 --> 00:07:37,588
I'll wait.

108
00:07:42,428 --> 00:07:48,307
The same ID, 676700b. That's the proof for
objective one: with nothing staged, the

109
00:07:48,347 --> 00:07:51,339
index describes the tree HEAD already has.

110
00:07:51,679 --> 00:07:57,743
Stage one change. One entry gets a
new blob ID: 4da99e3 becomes 0217758.

111
00:07:57,783 --> 00:08:04,102
The index now describes a different
tree, and git write-tree prints its ID.

112
00:08:04,442 --> 00:08:09,461
The commit's tree is dd3751c, the ID that
git write-tree printed before the commit

113
00:08:09,501 --> 00:08:14,336
existed. The commit didn't read the working
tree. Afterwards the index and HEAD

114
00:08:14,376 --> 00:08:18,655
describe the same tree again, which
is what "nothing to commit" means.

115
00:08:21,495 --> 00:08:26,760
The object list, then an edit. git hash-object
computes the ID that the edited file

116
00:08:26,800 --> 00:08:31,938
would get. No such object exists yet.
Predict what git add 🟢 SAFE changes in this

117
00:08:31,978 --> 00:08:32,258
list.

118
00:08:32,598 --> 00:08:37,685
After the add there's a fifth object, a
blob with the predicted ID, and the index

119
00:08:37,725 --> 00:08:40,851
entry names it. Two writes:
one object, one entry.

120
00:08:41,191 --> 00:08:45,574
Now the script edits the file
again, without adding it.

121
00:08:45,614 --> 00:08:51,927
Try this on paper, thirty seconds. Draw
three boxes: working tree, index, HEAD.

122
00:08:51,967 --> 00:08:56,270
In each box, write the last
line of the file it holds.

123
00:08:56,310 --> 00:09:02,945
Then predict what a commit made now would
contain. Pause me, and write your answer.

124
00:09:07,785 --> 00:09:11,511
MM says that both comparisons of
git status find a difference.

125
00:09:11,551 --> 00:09:16,309
git show :config/settings.yaml prints the
staged blob, which lacks debug: true.

126
00:09:16,349 --> 00:09:20,682
The commit records that blob, and the
file is still modified afterwards.

127
00:09:20,722 --> 00:09:25,723
Your three boxes held three different
versions, and the commit took the middle one.

128
00:09:25,763 --> 00:09:28,031
That's the CTO's question, reproduced.

129
00:09:28,371 --> 00:09:33,522
And the "unless": adding content that the
object database already holds creates no

130
00:09:33,562 --> 00:09:38,333
new object. Two index entries, one blob
ID, and the blob count stays at two.

131
00:09:38,673 --> 00:09:44,185
A slip: git add . picks up an environment
file, the dot env file where secrets such

132
00:09:44,225 --> 00:09:49,670
as API keys are kept. It's corrected at
once with git restore --staged, which is 🟡

133
00:09:49,710 --> 00:09:53,616
CAUTION: it changes an index
entry, and files on disk stay.

134
00:09:53,956 --> 00:09:59,685
Status is back to untracked. Predict:
is the secret anywhere in .git?

135
00:09:59,725 --> 00:10:01,942
Yes or no. Say it out loud.

136
00:10:06,782 --> 00:10:12,763
Yes. git fsck reports a dangling blob,
5620d7c, an object that nothing refers to,

137
00:10:12,803 --> 00:10:18,041
and git cat-file -p prints the secret
from it. The index entry is gone.

138
00:10:18,081 --> 00:10:22,650
The object that git add wrote is
not. Has it left the machine?

139
00:10:22,990 --> 00:10:27,451
No. The repository that received the
push has the commit and not the blob.

140
00:10:27,491 --> 00:10:28,668
You can breathe out.

141
00:10:30,508 --> 00:10:32,156
Five mistakes to watch for.

142
00:10:32,496 --> 00:10:37,608
Thinking the index is a list of changes.
Root cause: "staged changes" is the result

143
00:10:37,648 --> 00:10:42,449
of comparing the index with HEAD; the
index itself is a complete list of every

144
00:10:42,489 --> 00:10:43,256
tracked path.

145
00:10:43,596 --> 00:10:47,440
A fix that was tested is missing from the
commit. Root cause: the file was edited

146
00:10:47,480 --> 00:10:50,557
after the last git add, and the
commit is written from the index.

147
00:10:50,897 --> 00:10:55,051
Believing an empty "Changes to be
committed" means an empty index.

148
00:10:55,091 --> 00:10:59,818
Root cause: with nothing staged, the
index describes the same tree as HEAD.

149
00:11:00,158 --> 00:11:04,821
Assuming an unstaged secret left no trace.
Root cause: unstaging removes the entry,

150
00:11:04,861 --> 00:11:08,107
not the object; the blob remains
until garbage collection.

151
00:11:08,447 --> 00:11:13,248
Panicking that the unstaged secret was
pushed. Root cause: forgetting what a push

152
00:11:13,288 --> 00:11:18,208
sends: the objects that the pushed commits
need, and no commit refers to that blob.

153
00:11:20,048 --> 00:11:24,211
Now, out of the lab. An engineer on an
LLM application team runs git add .

154
00:11:24,251 --> 00:11:28,925
and notices in the status output that a 2
gigabyte checkpoint and an .env file were

155
00:11:28,965 --> 00:11:33,299
picked up. They unstage both immediately.
Two questions follow, and the model

156
00:11:33,339 --> 00:11:37,843
answers both. Is there history to rewrite?
No: a secret or a checkpoint that was

157
00:11:37,883 --> 00:11:41,308
added and then unstaged is in
no commit and hasn't travelled.

158
00:11:41,648 --> 00:11:47,183
Is it still on the laptop? Yes, as a stray
object, until garbage collection removes

159
00:11:47,223 --> 00:11:51,281
it once it's older than
gc.pruneExpire, two weeks by default.

160
00:11:51,321 --> 00:11:56,722
The same fact works in your favour on a
bad day: content that was staged once and

161
00:11:56,762 --> 00:11:59,207
then lost can be found with git fsck.

162
00:12:01,047 --> 00:12:05,917
Your turn. Do Lab 2.1, "The
three-trees prediction table", in

163
00:12:05,957 --> 00:12:09,298
lab-manual/m02-working-tree-index-head.md.

164
00:12:09,638 --> 00:12:14,124
The lab is a prediction exercise by design.
Fill in each row of the table, working

165
00:12:14,164 --> 00:12:18,596
tree, index and HEAD, before you run the
step, and mark every cell you got wrong.

166
00:12:18,636 --> 00:12:22,736
Those cells show you which sentence of
this video you haven't yet absorbed.

167
00:12:24,576 --> 00:12:30,479
Q27: "What exactly does git add write,
and what remains of it after git restore

168
00:12:30,519 --> 00:12:32,811
--staged? Does a push send it?"

169
00:12:35,151 --> 00:12:40,120
Answer out loud. A strong answer counts the
writes and names where each lands, says

170
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:45,129
which of them git restore --staged reverses
and which it doesn't, and reasons about

171
00:12:45,169 --> 00:12:50,078
the push from the rule for what a push
transfers. Say how long the leftover stays,

172
00:12:50,118 --> 00:12:51,285
and what removes it.

173
00:12:53,125 --> 00:12:58,412
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say, in your own words: the index is one

174
00:12:58,452 --> 00:13:03,419
file that lists every tracked path with
a mode and a blob ID, flat and sorted.

175
00:13:03,459 --> 00:13:06,244
It's a full snapshot,
not a list of changes.

176
00:13:06,584 --> 00:13:10,452
With nothing staged it describes the same
tree as HEAD, and I can prove that with

177
00:13:10,492 --> 00:13:14,456
git write-tree. git add writes a blob and
an index entry, as a copy at that moment,

178
00:13:14,496 --> 00:13:18,316
and later edits are not in it. git commit
records the index and doesn't read the

179
00:13:18,356 --> 00:13:18,943
working tree.

180
00:13:19,283 --> 00:13:23,816
Unstaging removes the entry and leaves
the blob, which no push sends and which

181
00:13:23,856 --> 00:13:25,751
garbage collection removes later.

182
00:13:27,591 --> 00:13:31,384
Read sections 5.1 to 5.3 of Chapter 5.

183
00:13:31,724 --> 00:13:37,966
Challenge: Exercise 2.4, Level 2,
"what does the commit contain?", in

184
00:13:38,006 --> 00:13:40,971
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

185
00:13:41,311 --> 00:13:45,678
Today you opened the index, and you
saw that a commit is built from it.

186
00:13:45,718 --> 00:13:48,907
Do the lab's prediction
table before the next video.

187
00:13:48,947 --> 00:13:53,562
Next time: three diffs, partial staging
with git add -p, and intent to add.

188
00:13:53,602 --> 00:13:56,791
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

189
00:13:56,831 --> 00:13:58,281
